From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECON/NANO/PHIL Defending Post-Scarcity

What will remain scarce even after the putative Nanotech Singularity?

Information. Software. 

Even with matter replicators, cheap manufacturing, whatever, the _inputs_,
the data, the programming, the designs, etc., will still be in demand.

And strong crypto is enough to keep secrets even from powerful,
technologically sophisticated opponents. Nothing in nanotech points to all
information becoming free (though conventional protection schemes using
patents, copyrights, etc, will likely change drastically).

This was my point in "Abundance is a Myth" about Hollywood: an entire
community which operates on "software." 

So I don't see any evidence for a "post-scarcity" world. Some things will
get very cheap, other things will not. So what else is new?

-Tim May
