Date: Mon, 8 Mar 93 04:07:33 MST
From: sneal@muskwa.ucs.ualberta.ca (Steve Neal)
Subject: QUESTION: Is writing more satisfying than conversation?

     Tim May, in his inimitable way, raises an interesting issue:
 
> [Comments summarized as
> "Face-to-face conversations sometimes bore the stink out of me."]
>
 
A couple of additional points to consider:
 
* Modern mores emphasize pointless conversation (i.e.  music,
weather) to the detriment of "conversation as the exchange of
specific information." In a social context, it is often considered
rude to enter a conversation with an "agenda" and/or to exit the
conversation once your agenda is complete.  In "polite"
conversation, you must exchange banalities until such time as you
find an excuse to fulfill your agenda, i.e.
 
     [handshakes and opening banalities elided]
     "This rain makes me sick."
     "Yeah, it makes me sick, too.  Say, speaking of sickness, has
      Hillary worked out the health care plan?"
 
as opposed to the "goal-driven" conversation, where you approach
your victim and cut right to the chase:
 
     "So, you loathsome hillbilly, has your vile wife figured out how
     to stick businesses for her peroxide bill?"
 
* Many people find blatant "conversation steering" to be rude or
awkward, again the result of social conditioning against "pushiness".
 
* Many topics are too complex for "real-time" conversation, in that
making an intelligent statement or coming up with an intelligent
question require a relatively long period of thought.  For a complex
topic, each statement may embody loads of assumptions and
implications which need to be checked, requiring a lot of processing
time.  This leads to conversations with long, pregnant pauses, which
again are felt to be impolite or awkward.  Inevitably, someone will
try to fill the dead air with a banality, a joke, a bad pun, or an
ill-considered "shot from the hip", which incites another one (or a
one-up), and so on.  (This is probably a major workness of the
parliamentary style of government; worse yet, if you don't say
anything, C-SPAN won't point the camera at you!).  In the worst case
scenario, someone will quote from "Monty Python and the Holy Grail",
which seems to be the standard conversation-wrecker in most of the
futurist-libertarian-SF circles I've been exposed to.
 
* My personal theory is that most email junkies are introverts by
nature, meaning that personal interaction tends to drain their
energy; it's not their "natural environment".  Such folk will tire of
conversation rather more quickly than their extroverted brethren.
(Interesting aside: a current psychological theory is that
introversion and extroversion are a function of brain structure and
chemistry.  Extroverts are "under-stimulated", and require high
stimulus/novelty levels for optimal brain function.  Introverts are
over-stimulated, and function best in low novelty environments.
I can't recall the reference for this, unfortunately.)
 
    <grin> Guess I won't be stopping by to chat next time I'm in
    the Bay area.
 
   -- Steve
 
