Subject: COMM: Usenet volume

Volume on Usenet is something like 60MB per day.

One thing I think is funny: the posting software we use prints
a warning to the effect that the article about to be posted
will cost "hundreds, if not thousands" of dollars to distribute
and whatnot... hmm:  news.lists tells me that there are about
30,000 articles per day.  Taking $200 as a low end of "hundreds,
if not thousands", that implies that usenet news costs about
six million dollars per day, or over two billion dollars per year.

Seems high to me!  Further, it's estimated that there are about
1.8 million news readers, making the overall cost (to somebody)
about $100/month per reader of usenet.  Ouch!

The "official" estimate of costs is something like 22 million
dollars per year all told (I couldn't find the actual figure,
so I computed it from other cost postings to news.lists; I might
have made an error, I suppose).  That works out to an average
cost of $2 per article (obviously, large binaries cost a great
deal more and short, informative letters correspondingly less).

Of course, a message saying

"WARNING:  You will be posting your article all over the world,
at tremendous cost to all involved (about $2.00).  Do you really
want to do it?"

wouldn't deter very much!

derek
