Date: Mon, 8 Mar 93 11:48:09 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: WELFARE: Welfare systems

   From: "Thomas D Burns" <TBURNS@gmuvax.gmu.edu>

   There have always been people at the bottom of the heap, but there
   has not always been an underclass, a community with no
   communityness. [...]

   How do we revive community? Perhaps I'm being simplistic (or
   boundlessly optimistic?) but it seems to me that people would do it
   on their own if the State wasn't stopping them. Maybe it won't be
   so easy, but surely not impossible? To me, the ghetto seems like a
   supersaturated social solution, poised to precipitate.

   Communities are self-organizing, self-reconstructing, like viruses.
   The State, by monopolizing the functions that PPL would fill,
   parasitizes community. Maybe a resistant strain will result.

As far as I can tell from history, it worked the other way.  The
Community has been around a long, long time, as a straightforward way
of solving various "tragedies of the commons" by tradition-based
mutual-aid systems, etc.  Of course, it worked because the people who
did not expect to benefit from the mutual-aid systems really didn't
have the option to leave -- people were not mobile, transportation was
poor, and it was hard to join a new community.

Then along came the railroad, the automobile, the suburbs, and the
nationwide job market.  Suddenly, the number of poor people living
near you became a significant determinant of your well-being -- too
many of them, and your "expected" "voluntary" (but not really
voluntary, if you wanted anyone to do business with you) contributions
to the welfare system became higher than what they would be in another
community with fewer poor people.

So people started "voting with their feet", that is, choosing which
community they would join in a very market-like way.  This caused
strong pressures on communities to reduce the amount of
welfare/mutual-aid that they would provide.  (Compare tax abatements
today!)

So the welfare system was re-organized on a statewide basis, and later
a federal basis, in order to provide welfare services that were not
vulnerable to this instability.  At the present time, there is still
sufficiently little emigration from the US to destabilize
federally-based welfare.

I see no way to solve this problem, as long as one can enter and leave
communities with relative freedom.  The only surviving mutual-aid
systems seem to be religious colonies, small towns, and the Mormons,
all of which have a very tight, closed social structure.

Dale

