Date: Mon, 8 Mar 93 11:18:16 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PHIL: Reincarnationists Not Deathists

I'm no reincarnationist, but let's give the doctrine a fair shake.

>P.S. If there is no death, then there is no risk, and any long-term
>thinking is unnecessary. 
>Krzys'

This is the second time I have heard this claim, and I don't think it
adequately represents the reincarnationist position.  

1) Death is still a risk.  A reincarnationist could worry that all human
life would be exterminated, leaving no human hardware to download into.  A
reincarnationist could also worry that all life would be exterminated,
leaving no hardware *at all* to download into.  (BTW, who is filling up all
the new hardware created by life's forward march?)

2) Death isn't the only risk.  Reincarnationists presumably value certain
kinds of lives over others.  They would thus prefer to see a world of
increasing wealth, for example.

Another misconception I've seen pop up: death is death, so
reincarnationists are deathists.  This begs the question at issue:  whether
or not identity can survive death of the body and transfer to new hardware.
 Nobody on *this* list should automatically assume that cessation of body
function = death! 

If you want to criticize reincarnationism, aim at its two major weaknesses:
 

1) It does not give a very plausible account of how identity is transfered
from body to body. 

2) It accepts a very, very low bandwidth of identity transfer.  How can you
say you live on in another body if you cannot recall your past, or can only
do so in a deep trance?  

